ping both visible; this distinguishes the past desire from the shame projected onto it.","mode":"symbolic","new_image":true,"reuse_image_id":null},{"id":"C06-B08","script_section":"S6","visual_information":"The photograph’s black wash divides into two separate charcoal forms, one marked by the former-self imprint and one by the present figure’s shadow; this shows that the embarrassing image and the wish behind it are not identical.","mode":"metaphor","new_image":true,"reuse_image_id":null},{"id":"C06-B09","script_section":"S6","visual_information":"The figure turns from the photograph toward its own oversized attached shadow while still holding the gold thread; this represents asking what the present self is trying to cut away.","mode":"symbolic","new_image":true,"reuse_image_id":null},{"id":"C06-B10","script_section":"S6","visual_information":"The figure gently pulls the photograph closer without restoring its erased portions; this shows that observation can occur without romanticizing the past.","mode":"metaphor","new_image":true,"reuse_image_id":null},{"id":"C06-B11","script_section":"S6","visual_information":"The old photograph rests beside the figure as a rough paper fragment rather than a portrait to admire or destroy; this reframes it as limited material for self-observation, not proof of a fixed cause.","mode":"symbolic","new_image":true,"reuse_image_id":null},{"id":"C06-B12","script_section":"S6","visual_information":"The figure and the former-self paper imprint stand on the same horizontal charcoal stroke, connected by one restrained gold line; this symbolizes placing the past within the continuity of personal history.","mode":"symbolic","new_image":true,"reuse_image_id":null},{"id":"C06-B13","script_section":"S6","visual_information":"The figure walks forward while the photograph remains behind with its shadow attached and the gold thread unbroken; this represents moving on without needing to love, erase, or fully condemn the past.","mode":"metaphor","new_image":true,"reuse_image_id":null}]}
